Build requirements for coole
----------------------------

- glibc or uClibc or musl
  - https://www.gnu.org/software/libc/
  - https://uclibc.org
  - https://www.musl-libc.org
- gcc or clang
  - https://gcc.gnu.org
  - https://clang.llvm.org
- meson >= 0.61 and ninja (on the machine that builds)
  - https://mesonbuild.com
  - https://ninja-build.org
- pkg-config
  - https://pkg-config.freedesktop.org/wiki/
- glib2 >= 2.58, with gmodule
  - https://www.gtk.org
  - Check the API baseline with: meson compile -C build check-glib-api
    (or sh maint/check-glib-api.sh).  This uses Docker to build and run
    the tests against the real GLib 2.58 headers and library from
    Debian 10.  Sources are mounted read only; build files and logs stay
    in a temporary directory.  Meson options can be passed to the script.
- S-Lang >= 2.0 or ncurses screen library
  - https://www.jedsoft.org/slang/
  - https://invisible-island.net/ncurses/ncurses.html
- gettext >= 0.18.2
  - https://www.gnu.org/software/gettext/

Optional:

- gpm (general purpose mouse daemon as an alternative to xterm mouse,
  Linux only)
  - https://www.nico.schottelius.org/software/gpm/
- X11 client library (reads the state of the keyboard modifiers when
  running in a terminal emulator under X11)
- Lua >= 5.3 (the Lua runtime plugin that runs editor scripts)
  - https://www.lua.org
- check >= 0.9.10 (unit tests)
  - https://libcheck.github.io/check/

Spell checking needs neither aspell nor hunspell at build time: the
built-in spell plugin opens libaspell or libhunspell at run time through
gmodule, and its settings dialog selects the engine and the language.


Installation instructions for coole
-----------------------------------

coole is built with meson, always in a build directory of its own,
outside the source tree.  A Git checkout and a release archive are built
the same way.

In short:

    meson setup build
    meson compile -C build
    meson test -C build           # optional, needs the check library
    meson install -C build

Several build directories can live side by side, each with its own
configuration:

    meson setup build-debug   --buildtype=debug
    meson setup build-release --buildtype=release
    meson setup build-asan    -Db_sanitize=address

The default build type is debugoptimized.  A debug build also turns on
the debug log (see COOLE_LOG_ENABLE in the manual page).

By default, "meson install" installs the files under /usr/local.  Use
--prefix to choose another place, and --sysconfdir for the system
configuration, e.g.

    meson setup build --prefix=/usr --sysconfdir=/etc

An existing build directory can be changed the same way.  The
directories are compiled into the program, so build it again as
yourself before installing; "sudo meson install" would otherwise
rebuild as root and leave root-owned files in the build directory:

    meson configure build --prefix=/usr --sysconfdir=/etc
    meson compile -C build
    sudo meson install -C build

"ninja -C build uninstall" (with sudo for a system directory) removes
the files the last "meson install" put in place and the directories it
created.

DESTDIR, or "meson install --destdir DIR", stages the installation in
another directory, as packages do.

"meson install" installs:

    $(bindir)/coole                       the program
    $(sysconfdir)/coole/                  keymaps, coole.menu, edit.indent.rc
    $(datadir)/coole/                     defaults.ini, charsets, help,
                                          skins, syntax, examples
    $(libdir)/coole/runtime-plugins/      the Lua runtime plugin
    $(datadir)/coole/lua/                 the Lua scripts
    $(mandir)/man1/coole.1                the manual page

The options of coole are given to "meson setup" as -Dname=value, and
can be changed later with "meson configure build -Dname=value".
"meson configure build" lists them all with their current values; the
meson built-in options (buildtype, prefix, b_sanitize, werror, ...) are
listed there as well.

-Dscreen=slang|ncurses
     Choose the library used to manage interaction with the terminal.
     S-Lang is the default, and it is tested better than ncurses.

-Dgpm=auto|enabled|disabled
     On GNU/Linux, coole uses the gpm library if it is found.  `enabled'
     makes a missing gpm an error, and `disabled' turns gpm mouse
     support off (e.g. to use the mouse only in terminal emulators).

-Dx11=auto|enabled|disabled
     By default, coole connects to the X Window System to query the
     status of the keyboard modifiers, such as Control, Shift and Alt,
     when it runs in a terminal emulator under X11.  This is necessary
     (but not always sufficient) to recognize some optional but handy
     key combinations like Ctrl-Home and Shift-Cursor keys.  With
     gmodule, libX11 is opened at run time and only its headers are
     needed to build.

-Dlua=auto|enabled|disabled
     The Lua runtime plugin (auto: when Lua 5.3 or newer and GModule
     are found).  `enabled' makes a missing Lua an error.

-Dspell=auto|enabled|disabled
     Spell checking through aspell or hunspell, opened at run time; it
     needs GModule.

-Dnls=auto|enabled|disabled
     Translations of the messages.

-Dtests=auto|enabled|disabled
     The unit tests (auto: when the check library is found, and never in
     a cross build).  They are run with "meson test".

-Dassert=true|false
     Keep the GLib assertions (on by default).

-Deditor_plugins_dir=DIR
     The directory where coole looks for dynamic editor plugins at run
     time [default=LIBDIR/coole/editor-plugins].  A dynamic editor
     plugin is a shared object that exports the
     mc_editor_plugin_register symbol; see doc/PLUGINS.

-Druntime_plugins_dir=DIR
     The directory of the runtime plugins, such as the Lua runtime
     [default=LIBDIR/coole/runtime-plugins].

-Dlua_scripts_dir=DIR
     The directory of the system Lua scripts
     [default=DATADIR/coole/lua/scripts].

-Dlua_modules_dir=DIR
     The directory of the system shared Lua modules
     [default=DATADIR/coole/lua/lib].

-Dwerror=true
     Handle all compiler warnings as errors (a meson built-in option).

A Lua installed in an unusual place is found through pkg-config: set
PKG_CONFIG_PATH to its lib/pkgconfig.  Compiler and linker flags of your
own go into CFLAGS, CPPFLAGS and LDFLAGS when "meson setup" runs, or
into -Dc_args and -Dc_link_args.


Maintainer targets
------------------

    meson compile -C build coole-pot         remake po/coole.pot
    meson compile -C build coole-update-po   merge the po/*.po files with it
    meson compile -C build indent            format the sources (clang-format)
    meson compile -C build man-in            remake doc/man/**/roff/*.1.in
                                             from the markdown (go-md2man)
    meson compile -C build buildcheck        what the ubuntu CI job does, in
                                             a container (Docker)
    meson dist -C build                      the release archive


Cross compiling
---------------

meson runs on the build machine and compiles for the target with the
toolchain named in a cross file, for instance for a little endian MIPS
router:

    [binaries]
    c = 'mipsel-linux-gcc'
    strip = 'mipsel-linux-strip'
    pkg-config = 'pkg-config'

    [host_machine]
    system = 'linux'
    cpu_family = 'mips'
    cpu = 'mipsel'
    endian = 'little'

    meson setup build-mipsel --cross-file mipsel.ini -Dlua=disabled

The unit tests are not built in a cross build.  PKG_CONFIG_LIBDIR (or
the pkg-config named in the cross file) must point at the pkg-config
files of the target's glib and screen library.  OpenWrt, Buildroot and
Yocto write the cross file themselves.


Terminal database
-----------------

There are many incomplete terminal databases out there, however, a
complete terminfo is bundled with ncurses.  (It is simple to generate
the termcap database using the infocmp utility in ncurses).

If you want to run coole on xterm/color_xterm/ansi_xterm (not rxvt),
then you might read contrib/README.xterm for further information.
